try (Connection conn = ds.getConnection();
     Statement stmt = conn.createStatement();
     ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery("SELECT * FROM users")) {

    ResultSetMetaData meta = rs.getMetaData();
    int columnCount = meta.getColumnCount();

    // Print column headers
    for (int i = 1; i <= columnCount; i++) {
        System.out.printf("%-20s %-15s%n",
            meta.getColumnName(i),
            meta.getColumnTypeName(i));
    }

    // Print rows
    while (rs.next()) {
        for (int i = 1; i <= columnCount; i++) {
            System.out.print(rs.getObject(i) + "\t");
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}
```

**Useful ResultSetMetaData methods:**
- `getColumnCount()` — number of columns
- `getColumnName(i)` — column name (1-indexed)
- `getColumnTypeName(i)` — SQL type name (VARCHAR, INTEGER, etc.)
- `getColumnType(i)` — java.sql.Types constant
- `isNullable(i)` — whether column allows nulls
- `getTableName(i)` — source table of the column

**The problem with primitive getters:**
SQL NULL and Java primitive 0 are different, but `getInt()` returns 0 for both.

```java
while (rs.next()) {
    // For String — null returned directly, easy
    String name = rs.getString("name"); // returns null if DB is NULL

    // For int — 0 returned for both 0 and NULL
    int age = rs.getInt("age");
    boolean isNull = rs.wasNull(); // must call immediately after getInt()

    if (isNull) {
        // age was NULL in DB
    } else {
        // age is the actual value
    }

    // Alternative: use getObject() for nullable primitives
    Integer ageBoxed = (Integer) rs.getObject("age"); // returns null if DB is NULL
}
```

**Rules:**
- `getString()`, `getObject()`: return `null` for SQL NULL ✓
- `getInt()`, `getDouble()`, `getBoolean()`: return 0/false for SQL NULL — use `wasNull()`
- `wasNull()` refers to the **last column read** — call immediately after the getter
